 * the Worker's list); the title, heading, kicker and description lead with
 * fitted wardrobes, then built-in shelving (the reasons are in areas.ts); the
 * opening carries "bespoke carpentry" and "bespoke joinery" once each; a line
 * drawing beside each section that describes something
 * (src/data/area-drawings.ts: villas across a canal, a curved stucco terrace,
 * wardrobes and cupboards around a bed), none where a section is rules or
 * access only; the guide prices as cards; register photos and one labelled AI
 * image (ssp-058, cropped to 3:2, AI panelled wardrobes with leather pulls,
 * Figma node 38:2, and since 21 Nov 2026 an AI sage bookcase in place of
 * ssp-057, which showed the same bookcase as ssp-058; areas.ts says why).
 *
 * Sources. Maida Vale is in the City of Westminster, so nothing here is
 * Camden's. Every local fact from the ops facts file
 * research/area-pages-2026-11-14/facts-maida-vale-2026-11-14.md, read
 * 14 Nov 2026:
 *   Westminster's Maida Vale Conservation Area Information Leaflet (May 2004,
 *     "S1"): the Regent's Canal and the Grand Union Canal set the earliest
 *     layout; building got under way in the 1830s and the south was
 *     virtually complete by the mid-1860s; Little Venice, "white stucco,
 *     greenery and water"; early mansion blocks around Elgin Avenue; over 350
 *     listed buildings, mostly Grade II, around Warwick Avenue, Warrington
 *     Crescent, Formosa Street and Clifton Gardens (its 2004 count, stated as
 *     its own with the year).
 *   Westminster's Conservation Areas Character Overview, entry 27 ("S2"):
 *     "The imposing stucco crescents of Warrington Crescent and Formosa
 *     Street are the most notable features".
 *   The Victoria County History, Middlesex vol 9, Paddington: Maida Vale
 *     (1989, "S3", used for dates and building types only): stuccoed villas
 *     on the southern avenues; the first flats planned in 1890 at Nos 1 and 3
 *     Maida Vale; Carlton, Elgin and Ashworth Mansions by 1902.
 *   Historic England, Nos 2 to 18 Formosa Street (list entry 1211906, "S4"):
 *     a mid-19th century terrace of three storeys in brick and stucco, upper
 *     sash windows "in architraves with cornices on consoles", Grade II.
 *   Westminster's listed buildings and listed building consent pages ("S5";
 *     the consent page re-read 17 Nov for its Certificate of Lawfulness line,
 *     "to establish if proposed works to a listed building would be lawful"),
 *     the Planning Portal's internal alterations page ("S6"), Westminster's
